VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'updates were all I was going to show you in this video, that would be awesome . But actually, I have something else that I really want to show you. So, when you\'re working on a'

[11:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'film project that uses artificial intelligence, if you rely solely on AI, the results may lack human connection. This is not always the case, and of course, with'

[11:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'iterations and lots of hints, you can usually achieve what you want. But my favorite way to interact with AI tools is to use a hybrid workflow, where you'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'film real actors and then transfer their performance to a new environment or project it onto a new character, using it as a kind of motion capture. So I want to show you some of the different tests we\'ve'

[11:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'been doing recently and talk about what I think it means for the future of location cinema. This video clip that I\'m showing you now is of an actress named Joel. Joel was a member of the'

[11:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Curious Refuge community and volunteered to help us with this shoot. As you can see, we used very even lighting and a gray pavilion for our shoots. We have a gray background with'

[12:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tracking points so the camera can latch onto something. This is very important for parallax. If it were just a white or green background, it would be harder for the AI ​​system to find tracking points to understand what the camera shake should look like'

[12:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'relative to the environment. So you see we have a very clear Joel game . When we were on set , the idea was that Joel is supposedly in a dystopian'

[12:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'post-apocalyptic world, she hides behind a car, looks around, and then looks down . I have no idea, she\'s just having a terrible day here. So, inside Magnific, I uploaded our original video footage. We call this “gray'

[12:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'boxing,” and there is some debate about whether to use this method or whether it is better to just go to the location and shoot some version of your scene. I guess the short answer is: it probably doesn\'t'

[12:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'matter, but you have more control over the lighting and environment if you\'re working in a pavilion or semi-professional venue. If you go on location, you will of course have to deal with all the limitations and logistical'

[13:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'issues, but at the same time you will get the benefit of a real visual perception of the scene during the filming process. So it really depends on the genre you\'re working with, but we\'re continuing to run tests and will share the results in'

[13:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'future videos. For our example, I uploaded the material and gave a request: replace the background in the video with a cinematic post-apocalyptic environment. Of course, I could be more specific about what I want to see, whether it\'s a car, an'

[13:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment, or some aliens walking around. It really depends on the goal, but for this test I wanted to keep things pretty general. I also noted that we want to replace the blue boxes on the damaged machine, and we click "'

[13:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generate". A few minutes later we received this video. Wow. This is just madness. Can we'

[14:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
